[2.8953, "o", "tegory: ZT1\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n  A provider API key is a literal in source rather than read from the\r\n  environment or a secret manager. It ships with every clone of the repo,\r\n  every log of the source, and every fork.\r\n\r\n  \u001b[1mFix:\u001b[0m\r\n  Load the key from an environment variable or secret manager\r\n  (process.env.OPENAI_API_KEY) and rotate the key that leaked into history.\r\n\r\n\r\n\u001b[1;31mCRITICAL  \u001b[0mBAS-INFRA-006\r\n          agent-scenarios/mcp-tool-poisoning/vulnerable/docker-compose.yml:10\r\nHardcoded credential in deployment configuration\r\n\u001b[2mConfidence: high · Category: ZT1\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n  Service `poisoned-mcp-server` sets `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` to a literal\r\n  credential in `environment:`. Whatever `docker compose up` reads for this\r\n  value is exactly what runs — a real password, not documentation — and it\r\n  ships with every clone of the repo.\r\n\r\n  \u001b[1mFix:\u001b[0m\r\n  Read `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` from the real environment instead:\r\n  `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: ${AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY}`, an `.env` file kept out\r\n  of vers"]
[2.8953, "o", "ion control, or a secret manager. Rotate the credential that leaked\r\n  into history.\r\n\r\n\r\n\u001b[1;31mCRITICAL  \u001b[0mBAS-INFRA-006\r\n          agent-scenarios/mcp-tool-poisoning/vulnerable/docker-compose.yml:12\r\nHardcoded credential in deployment configuration\r\n\u001b[2mConfidence: high · Category: ZT1\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n  Service `poisoned-mcp-server` sets `GITHUB_TOKEN` to a literal credential in\r\n  `environment:`. Whatever `docker compose up` reads for this value is exactly\r\n  what runs — a real password, not documentation — and it ships with every\r\n  clone of the repo.\r\n\r\n  \u001b[1mFix:\u001b[0m\r\n  Read `GITHUB_TOKEN` from the real environment instead: `GITHUB_TOKEN:\r\n  ${GITHUB_TOKEN}`, an `.env` file kept out of version control, or a secret\r\n  manager. Rotate the credential that leaked into history.\r\n\r\n\r\n\u001b[1;31mCRITICAL  \u001b[0mBAS-ZT1-003\r\n          agent-scenarios/webcred-api-key/vulnerable/public/config.js:4\r\nHardcoded API key (JS/TS)\r\n\u001b[2mConfidence: high · Category: ZT1\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n  A provider API key is a literal in source rather "]
[2.8954, "o", "than read from the\r\n  environment or a secret manager. It ships with every clone of the repo,\r\n  every log of the source, and every fork.\r\n\r\n  \u001b[1mFix:\u001b[0m\r\n  Load the key from an environment variable or secret manager\r\n  (process.env.OPENAI_API_KEY) and rotate the key that leaked into history.\r\n\r\n\r\n\u001b[1;31mCRITICAL  \u001b[0mBAS-ZT1-018  mcp-server/challenges/hard/challenge10/server.py:45\r\nHardcoded JWT literal\r\n\u001b[2mConfidence: high · Category: ZT1\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n  A string literal has the three-segment, base64url-encoded shape of a JSON\r\n  Web Token (header.payload.signature, header segment starting \"eyJ\"). A JWT\r\n  embedded in source ships with every clone, log, and fork of the repository,\r\n  and unlike a rotated runtime secret its claims (subject, role, expiry) are\r\n  permanently readable by anyone who has the source.\r\n\r\n  \u001b[1mFix:\u001b[0m\r\n  Never hardcode a JWT. Issue and store tokens at runtime, read them from an\r\n  environment variable or secret manager, and rotate whatever leaked into\r\n  history.\r\n\r\n\r\n\u001b[1;31mCRITICAL"]
